  - [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m → the stored id, or [38;5;153mnull[39m
  - [38;5;153msetUserId(id)[39m → persists it
  - [38;5;153mclearUserId()[39m → removes it (this is the logout half you asked for)
  - [38;5;153mUSER_ID_KEY[39m → the storage key constant, so nothing hardcodes the string

  Every [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m call is wrapped in [38;5;153mtry[39m/[38;5;153mcatch[39m with an in-memory fallback. Safari private mode and storage-disabled
  browsers [3mthrow[0m on access rather than returning null, and an identity layer that throws would take the login flow down
  with it.

  [1m[38;5;153mapp.js[0m becomes a module and changes in three places: it imports from [38;5;153msession.js[39m; the stub [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, [39m
  [38;5;153muserId, user }[39m instead of [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m, with a comment marking [38;5;153muserId[39m as the field the real API will supply; and
  the submit handler calls [38;5;153msetUserId(result.userId)[39m on success, then logs the userId as the "who logged in" record.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[0m changes one attribute — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m.

  [1mError handling.[0m If the response has no [38;5;153muserId[39m, nothing is stored and a warning is logged; login's success/failure
  reporting is unchanged. Storage failures degrade to in-memory silently. Neither case can break the form.

  [1mNot touched:[0m [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, [38;5;153mREADME.md[39m.

  Two open questions inside this section:

  Does the design look right so far? And on those two: I'd default to [1mno logout button[0m (ship [38;5;153mclearUserId()[39m and wire a
  button when there's a UI story for it) and to [1mpreserving [38;5;153muser[0m — dropping a field from a return shape is the kind of
  thing that breaks a caller I can't see.

